{:doc
 "RATCHET — known divergences for the scriptc target. Shrink-only; see
  known-divergences-js.edn for the schema and bin/beagle-certify for the gate.
  Keyed by [:id :bucket]. Categories: :bug (tracked defect, carries :thread) |
  :host-model (target-inherent gap) | :strictness (beagle intentionally
  stricter). No commas (dual EDN/Racket-readable).

  Every entry below is a CURRENT PRODUCT GAP, not an inapplicable row. The
  scriptc rows are declared at the behavior beagle owes — a `static` row must
  compile 100% statically, a `native` row must also match Node — so the gap
  fires as a classified bucket instead of being hidden behind a weaker row
  kind. Closing a gap turns its entry STALE and the gate fails until the entry
  is deleted (and the golden regenerated) in the same commit.

  Upstream ScriptC (Apache-2.0, rev 20c3a6c27da4807f607ebe496663842b67e87f0e)
  is used only as a black-box executable oracle: it compiles async/await,
  records/arrays, Map and Set statically with native parity, which is why the
  rows below are :bug (beagle owes the lowering) and not :host-model."
 :entries
 [
  ;; --- validator: target-neutral forms rejected at the top level ----------
  {:id "sc-record" :bucket :compile-fail :category :bug
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "defrecord is rejected as an unsupported top-level form. Upstream
          ScriptC compiles records/arrays 35/35 statically, so this is a beagle
          admission gap. Delete when typed record lowering + admission land."}

  {:id "sc-map-set-literal" :bucket :compile-fail :category :bug
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "Typed top-level defs of map/set literals are rejected by the scriptc
          validator. Upstream ScriptC is 50/50 on Map and 47/47 on Set, so this
          is admission, not a host limit. Delete when the validator admits them
          and the native row proves value semantics."}

  {:id "sc-comment-neutral" :bucket :compile-fail :category :bug
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "A target-neutral top-level (comment ...) parses to nil and the
          validator rejects it — a false rejection of a form every other target
          erases. Delete when comments are erased/accepted for scriptc."}

  {:id "sc-target-case" :bucket :compile-fail :category :bug
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "target-case picks the :scriptc branch correctly INSIDE an admitted
          defn, but a top-level def carrying one is rejected, so branch
          selection is unreachable at the top level. Delete when branch
          selection is pinned across top-level contexts."}

  ;; --- async boundary types are not admitted by the scriptc validator -----
  {:id "sc-await-reject" :bucket :compile-fail :category :bug
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "RECONCILED at 519cd6fe629aa6d1165f044d9f8134e0c034b596: the
          JS-family gate this row originally pinned is CLOSED — check.rkt now
          admits js/await for scriptc, so the old `js/await is only supported
          in beagle/js` message is gone. The row still compile-fails one layer
          deeper, in emit-scriptc's validator: `unsupported boundary type
          (type-app 'Promise (list (type-prim 'Int))); experimental scriptc
          supports primitive function boundaries only`. Upstream ScriptC
          compiles async/await 15/15 statically with native stdout/exit parity,
          so this stays implementation scope, never an inapplicable row. Delete
          when the validator admits Promise-typed boundaries."}

  ;; --- host model: f64-backed scalars ------------------------------------
  {:id "sc-fixed-width-reject" :bucket :compile-fail :category :host-model
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "TEMPORARY host-model ratchet, not an inapplicable row: TypeScript's
          number and ScriptC's native C double are both f64, so an I32/U64
          boundary has no faithful representation yet. The row stays declared
          as `static` (the behavior beagle owes) so a representation decision
          — boxed fixed-width carriers, or a documented narrowing — turns this
          entry STALE and forces its deletion."}

  ;; --- diagnostics that reject correctly but are not user-facing ---------
  {:id "sc-missing-return-annot" :bucket :diag-unpointed :category :bug
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "D8. The defn rejects, but the message is `unsupported boundary type
          #f` — it prints an internal absent-type value instead of naming the
          missing `:-` return annotation. Delete when the diagnostic names
          `:-`."}

  {:id "sc-untyped-def" :bucket :diag-unpointed :category :bug
   :thread "@019fa49d-1276-71d6-a925-7b42ca81ff2a"
   :note "D9. The top-level def rejects by printing the raw def-form struct at
          the user. Delete when the validator emits a pointed user-facing
          message (and again, as a compile-fail entry, if top-level def stays
          unimplemented for longer)."}
 ]}
